	Annotation {
	  SID			  "191"
	  Name			  "IMPORTANT! This force scaling is required to ensure that the forces are set to zero when the position con"
	  "troller is \"Off\". \nAlthough it is in theory possible to update the position controller to the current hand posit"
	  "ion at each time step (which \nin theory produces 0 force from the position controller), this approach does not wor"
	  "k with a PMAC-based KINARM.\n(If your robot computer has wide grey ribbon cables coming out the back, then you have"
	  " a PMAC-based KINARM).\nThe problem with PMAC based KINARMs is noise that is created in the virtual_postion, which "
	  "is caused by the PMAC sampling \nat 1129 Hz and the model running at 2 kHz (i.e. data are re-sampled). These differ"
	  "ences mean that time base used by the \nposition controller to calculate velocity is incorrect. It should be 884 us"
	  ", but instead is 500 us. As such, at 2nd or third frame you \ncan see the position may hold. This re-sampling leads"
	  " to high instantaneous velocities intermingled with v = 0. There is no way\naround this on a PMAC system. Therefore"
	  " you need to disable the position controller's forces if you want the subject to move freely"
	  Position		  [565, 340]
	  HorizontalAlignment	  "left"
	  DropShadow		  on
	  ZOrder		  -1
	  FontSize		  12
	}
	Annotation {
	  SID			  "123"
	  Name			  "In the Position Controller dialog, the \"Time base of desired Position input signal\" was set to 0.001.\n"
	  "This value is different from the Simulink sample-time of 0.0005 for that signal because the calculations \nthat cre"
	  "ate that signal in the Create_Trajectory block are based on 1 kHz. Please see the Help for \nPosition Controller bl"
	  "ock for more details."
	  Position		  [269, 260]
	  ZOrder		  -1
	}
      }
    }
